WEBVTT

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.889
Question for you, have you ever played the game "Telephone"? You know,

00:00:07.889 --> 00:00:10.303
the game where someone whispers something to someone,

00:00:10.303 --> 00:00:12.536
and then that person whispers it to someone else,

00:00:12.536 --> 00:00:14.768
and then that person whispers it to someone else.

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:17.000
And by the time you get through everyone playing,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.200
The original message is all messed up.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.048
I used to kind of dread that game. Most people seemed to like it,

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:27.078
but somehow whenever it reached me, the message was always really messed up,

00:00:27.078 --> 00:00:29.200
so I felt like I was spreading nonsense.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.300
Well, either that or it was me all along that misheard it.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.400
Anyway, the game is all about communication and how things spread.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.876
Communication is incredibly important, not just for us,

00:00:39.876 --> 00:00:44.958
but for the things that we are made up of, like our cells. Cells make up all the things.

00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:49.000
And while they don't talk in the way that you and I do, it's important

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.800
for their messages, their signaling to be transmitted and received appropriately.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.176
Multicellular organisms need their many cells to be able to work together to carry out functions.

00:01:00.176 --> 00:01:04.572
Just consider all the cells working together in one of your organs, like your heart,

00:01:04.572 --> 00:01:05.200
for example.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.598
We're going to be talking a lot about receptors. A receptor is a molecule,

00:01:10.598 --> 00:01:13.900
such as a protein, where a signal molecule can bind.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.000
place you can find receptors is on the surface of a cell membrane.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.370
When a signal molecule binds the receptor, amazing things can happen.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.689
The receptor might start activating another molecule for an action to happen.

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:31.400
The receptor often changes its shape slightly in the process. More about that later.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Receptor, signal molecule.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.700
The signal molecule can be considered a ligand.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:43.371
which basically means that it's the smaller molecule that binds to a typically larger molecule.

00:01:43.371 --> 00:01:50.500
Signal molecules can be a variety of things such as gas molecules or hydrophobic biomolecules like lipids or

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.678
Hydrophilic biomolecules like some kinds of proteins,

00:01:53.678 --> 00:01:57.100
but ligands are generally smaller than the receptors they bind.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.300
Ligands and receptors can have a very specific fit as well.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.900
Let's talk about the general sequence of cell signaling.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.700
First reception.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.000
a signal molecule binds a receptor.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.788
Second, transduction. The receptor gets activated by this binding.

00:02:13.788 --> 00:02:16.536
This often means the receptor will change its shape.

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:23.300
It could even involve a whole series of molecules changing their conformation in something called a signal transduction pathway.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.100
This can amplify the original signal.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.800
Third, response.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.019
There's some kind of response that is going to happen.

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:35.143
A portion of DNA that is found in the nucleus getting transcribed, for example,

00:02:35.143 --> 00:02:36.500
that's a type of response.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.403
Now cell signaling can involve intracellular signaling,

00:02:40.403 --> 00:02:42.500
which occurs within the cell itself,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.900
intercellular signaling, where a cell communicates with another cell.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.562
In many cases, signaling involves both,

00:02:49.562 --> 00:02:53.100
signaling between cells and then also signaling within cells.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.400
Now when we're talking about signals traveling from one cell to another,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.100
Distance matters.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.200
Some cells are close and have direct contact.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.731
In the case of these two animal cells, they are gap junction close.

00:03:06.731 --> 00:03:10.984
Gap junctions in these animal cells, or plasmodesmata in plant cells,

00:03:10.984 --> 00:03:15.300
are connections between two close together cells that allow ions or...

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.801
other small molecules to pass, and by doing so,

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:20.200
they don't have to pass across the cell membrane.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.800
Paracrine signaling allows a cell to target another cell by a signal molecule that may diffuse between them.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.370
These cells are still close but need not be connected.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:35.300
The ligands in paracrine signaling tend to be rapidly absorbed and rapidly degraded.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.800
The ligands are typically not traveling far, as this is local signaling.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.600
Synaptic signaling, which specifically involves neurotransmitters in a synapse,

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.400
is another example of this local signaling.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.800
And what about long distance?

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:55.200
Endocrine signaling can allow a cell to communicate with a target cell from far away.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.700
signals may be carried in the bloodstream.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.900
Hormones released by certain types of endocrine cells are a great example.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.719
We also want to point out that a cell could just signal itself. For example,

00:04:06.719 --> 00:04:08.026
in autocrine signaling,

00:04:08.026 --> 00:04:14.107
a cell could secrete a certain type of molecule which then binds to its own receptor and causes a response.

00:04:14.107 --> 00:04:15.300
A cell releasing its—

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.900
own growth factor could be an example of this.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.800
Let's just give a few signaling examples so we can get a basic understanding of the vocab and what this can look like.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.500
Let's say we have a steroid hormone that travels through the cell's semipermeable membrane.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.600
Remember, that would mean the steroid hormone is our signal molecule, our ligand.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:40.400
Once inside the cell, it binds to a protein receptor within the cell.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.067
The protein receptor is active.

00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:47.400
The protein receptor travels into the nucleus where the cell's DNA is found.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:53.272
This protein receptor binds to DNA and is involved in getting transcription of a certain gene going,

00:04:53.272 --> 00:04:56.500
which eventually can be used to produce a specific protein.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.700
This is an example of signaling inside the cell.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:00.800
simplified a bit.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.400
In the example, the receptor was inside the cell.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.500
a cytoplasmic receptor.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.800
But it doesn't have to be. Receptors are frequently part of the cell's membrane surface.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.975
If a receptor is sitting outside of the cell membrane surface,

00:05:14.975 --> 00:05:17.248
then the ligand doesn't have to come in. In fact,

00:05:17.248 --> 00:05:21.468
there could be properties of the ligand that may not allow it to pass through the membrane.

00:05:21.468 --> 00:05:24.900
The ligand could be hydrophilic, which would make it hard to pass through.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.000
Let's mention an example of a cell surface receptor type, a ligand-gated ion channel.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.454
Ions normally don't go unassisted through the cell membrane. They are charged after all,

00:05:36.454 --> 00:05:41.100
see our cell transport video, but a ligand-gated ion channel gives them a way through.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.300
through a channel.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:46.418
But it is controlled. In this example, the channel is closed.

00:05:46.418 --> 00:05:50.440
But here comes a signal molecule, a ligand. It binds the receptor,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.000
which is the channel protein in this case.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.900
Now the channel protein responds by opening.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.000
The ions, not the ligand, go through.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.300
Once ions get through, the concentration of ions increases in the cell.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.800
And you may wonder, okay, so now there are ions in the cell. Why does that matter?

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.913
Well, the increasing ion concentration can trigger a cellular response.

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:15.527
After reaching a certain concentration and stimulating a cell response,

00:06:15.527 --> 00:06:19.700
the ligand can leave its binding site from the receptor and the channel can close.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.015
Ligand-gated ion channels can be used by neurons.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:26.519
A neurotransmitter may be the ligand for the channel to open.

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.300
This could happen in a synapse.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.557
But just be aware that not all ion channels are ligand-gated ion channels.

00:06:33.557 --> 00:06:36.200
Ion channels can be gated by other things.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.100
A voltage-gated ion channel, for example, depends on electrical membrane potential.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.300
Not a ligand

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.977
By the way, voltage-gated ion channels are used by neurons too.

00:06:45.977 --> 00:06:48.600
Check out Action Potential in Neurons to learn more.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.500
There are other types of cell membrane surface receptors that we don't have time to go into in this video.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:01.100
G protein linked receptors and enzyme linked receptors are two other types that we encourage you to explore.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.300
So in summary, why do we care about the cell signaling thing?

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:06.069
Well,

00:07:06.069 --> 00:07:11.400
realize that your body processes that keep you alive rely on your cell's ability of cell signaling.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:19.876
From the regulation of your heartbeat to the hormone signals traveling long distances in your body to the way neurons in your brain communicate,

00:07:19.876 --> 00:07:22.400
your multicellular self needs cell signaling.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.500
But there are many disorders where cell signaling does not work as it should.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:33.100
And so understanding all of the complexities of cell signaling is critical in order to find ways to treat them.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:38.600
Cancer is an example of a disease that can involve body cells with problems in cell signaling.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.900
We mentioned autocrine signaling.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.900
The cancer cell could have a problem where it produces too much of its own growth factor.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.600
causing excessive cellular division.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.600
Cancer cells can also have many other cell signaling difficulties where they do not function like normal healthy cells.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:59.000
Or another example, there are also pathogens.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.800
such as viruses or bacteria that can take advantage of cell signaling.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.700
Consider the virus HIV, which targets helper T cells.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:14.500
Helper T cells are important to immune cells in your body and they have something called a CD4 receptor on their surface.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:20.100
That receptor is so important so that helper T cells can communicate with other immune cells.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.932
but as mentioned in our viruses video, HIV targets that CD4 receptor.

00:08:24.932 --> 00:08:33.800
It is because of the virus binding to that receptor on helper T cells that the virus can attach and infect the cell in the first place.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.500
a fact that continues to be researched for treatment options.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.900
the understanding of the details of cell signaling continues to expand.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.100
That's it for the Amoeba Sisters and we remind you to stay curious.
